Cap the screencast capture rate for monitor/window sources, not just virtual.

xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland streams at panel rate when the consumer sets no
framerate constraint -- 180Hz on DP-1, 240Hz on eDP-1 here -- while a WFD stream
only ever carries 30fps. That makes the portal do roughly 6x redundant
screencopy work: it pauses/resumes the PipeWire stream about 10 times a second,
tearing down and re-adding its whole buffer pool each cycle, renegotiating the
identical format thousands of times, until it exhausts the pool:

  [WARN] [pipewire] Asked for a wl_shm buffer which is legacy.
  [ERR]  [screencopy] tried scheduling on already scheduled cb (type 0)
  [LOG]  [screencopy/pipewire] Out of buffers
  [LOG]  [sc] Retrying screencopy (1/10)

Once the retries run out the portal stops delivering frames, pipewiresrc's
create() blocks forever, and the stream silently freezes on its last frame while
RTP keeps flowing (hyprwm/xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland#180).

GND already caps max-framerate at 30/1 for ND_SCREEN_CAST_SOURCE_TYPE_VIRTUAL.
The monitor and window paths had no srcfilter at all; this gives them the same
constraint. Capping it via xdph's screencopy:max_fps instead does fix the
symptom (measured: "Out of buffers" and the wl_shm warnings both drop to zero,
throughput goes from a frozen 1-3 kbit/s to ~4.5 Mbit/s sustained) but that
option is not per-client, so it would also cap OBS, gpu-screen-recorder and
browser screen sharing to 30fps.

Only max-framerate is constrained. Capture stays at native resolution --
videoscale does the downscale -- and framerate itself stays variable (0/1),
which is what the portal advertises. Pinning framerate instead fails
negotiation, since nothing in this chain converts framerate.

Both source-bin implementations need it: app/nd-window.c for the GUI and
stream/nd-stream.c for the daemon. They are near-identical copies and patching
only one silently changes nothing for the other.

Not upstreamed.

--- a/src/app/nd-window.c
+++ b/src/app/nd-window.c
@@ -190,7 +190,30 @@
       gst_element_link_many (src, filter, dst, NULL);
     }
   else
-    gst_element_link_many (src, dst, NULL);
+    {
+      /* Cap the capture rate the same way the virtual-display path already
+       * does. Without this the portal streams at panel rate -- 180Hz on DP-1,
+       * 240Hz on eDP-1 here -- while WFD only ever carries 30fps, so
+       * xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland does ~6x redundant screencopy work, tears
+       * its buffer pool down ~10x/sec, runs out ("Out of buffers" ->
+       * "Retrying screencopy") and eventually stops delivering frames.
+       * Constrain only max-framerate: the capture stays at native resolution
+       * (videoscale handles the downscale) and framerate itself stays variable,
+       * which is what the portal advertises. Doing it here rather than via
+       * xdph's global screencopy:max_fps keeps other screencast clients -- OBS,
+       * gpu-screen-recorder, browsers -- at full rate. */
+      caps = gst_caps_new_simple ("video/x-raw",
+                                  "max-framerate", GST_TYPE_FRACTION, 30, 1,
+                                  NULL);
+      filter = gst_element_factory_make ("capsfilter", "srcfilter");
+      gst_bin_add (bin, filter);
+      g_object_set (filter,
+                    "caps", caps,
+                    NULL);
+      g_clear_pointer (&caps, gst_caps_unref);
+
+      gst_element_link_many (src, filter, dst, NULL);
+    }

   res = gst_element_factory_make ("intervideosrc", "screencastsrc");
   g_object_set (res,
